# Repo-wide ignores.
#
# GitHub hard-rejects any single file over 100 MB, and the rejection happens at
# push time — after the commit, when the fix is a history rewrite rather than a
# `git rm`. Everything below is here to stop a large or unpublishable file being
# committed in the first place.

# --- large reference material -------------------------------------------
# Scanned textbooks and similar. Kept on disk, never committed: they are far
# over GitHub's file size limit, and this repo is public, so a copyrighted
# scan should not be published from it.
References/**/*Full Textbook*.pdf

# --- installers and binaries ---------------------------------------------
# Downloaded installers are not source. Re-download them instead.
References/*.exe
*.msi
*.dmg
*.iso

# --- build output ---------------------------------------------------------
# A CMake build tree at the repo root was tracked by accident (58 files:
# CMakeCache.txt with machine-specific absolute paths, compiled binaries, and
# a FetchContent clone of nlohmann/json). That clone carried its own .git, so
# `git add -A` recorded it as a *submodule reference* with no matching entry
# in .gitmodules — which made `git submodule update --init` fail outright with
# "No url found for submodule path 'build/_deps/json-src'".
#
# Projects/Hardware-Check/.gitignore already ignores `build/`, but that rule
# is scoped to that folder and does not reach a build tree at the root.
/build/

# --- python --------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# Virtual environments. The project ones now live together under
# "Virtual Environments/", which has its own .gitignore doing the work — it
# ignores everything in that folder except Requirements/. These two catch the
# conventional names anywhere else in the tree, since git cannot recognise a
# venv by its contents and an unlisted one dumps thousands of files into
# `git status`.
.venv/
venv/

# --- node ----------------------------------------------------------------
node_modules/

# --- env / secrets -------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# --- editors / os --------------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# --- graphify knowledge graph --------------------------------------------
# Build output, not source. graph.json is ~2.7 MB and graph.html ~2.2 MB, and
# both are rewritten wholesale whenever any indexed file changes — committing
# them would put a multi-megabyte binary-ish diff in front of every ordinary
# code commit. The graph is fully reproducible from the tree it describes:
#   graphify extract . --code-only     (local AST, no API key, no token cost)
# .graphifyignore IS committed: it defines the scope, and is the part worth
# sharing between machines.
graphify-out/*
#
# ...except the two things in there that are NOT reproducible from source.
# memory/ is saved Q&A results and reflections/ is the digest built from them:
# knowledge earned by working in this repo, not output derived from it. Losing
# them on a fresh clone loses the accumulated context, which is the whole point.
!graphify-out/memory/
!graphify-out/reflections/
